    "summary": "Smoking Dark Star → Morning Dew and tight Two Souls in Communion",
    "blurb": "The London show delivers a blistering Dark Star → Morning Dew that showcases Jerry’s soaring leads and Phil’s deep bass, plus a standout Two Souls in Communion. The second set stays hot with solid renditions of He's Gone, Sugar Magnolia and Comes A Time. Overall the performance is high‑energy and captured on excellent soundboard sources.",
    "review": "May 23, 1972 at the Strand Lyceum finds the Dead firing on all cylinders during what many fans now call one of the Europe ’72 highlights. The band launches into a smoking Dark Star that morphs seamlessly into an emotionally charged Morning Dew – Jerry’s guitar accents sparkle while Phil lays down a rumbling, warm foundation that drives the improvisation forward. Later in the set, “The Stranger (Two Souls in Communion)” stands out as a soulful interlude where Pigpen’s organ swells and Keith adds a rag‑time texture that perfectly complements the group’s groove. The second half stays tight and inspired, with solid takes on He's Gone, Sugar Magnolia and a beautifully lyrical Comes A Time, all wrapped up by a rolling Not Fade Away/Hey Bo Diddley medley. Across six soundboard recordings the mix is clear, stereo and remarkably free of drop‑outs, making this show an essential listen for anyone exploring the peak of the early‑’70s Dead.",
